# The build context, as an allow-list: exclude everything, then put back the
# few paths the release build in Dockerfile actually reads.
#
# This way round on purpose. A deny-list grows a hole every time a directory
# appears at the root of the tree — the new one is in the context until someone
# remembers to add it — while an allow-list fails closed: a path nobody named
# is simply not shipped to the daemon.
*

!Cargo.toml
!Cargo.lock
!rust-toolchain.toml

# The directory and everything under it. Both lines, so that the walk descends
# into it whichever way the matcher reads a bare directory name.
!src
!src/**

# The schema is read at *compile* time — `sqlx::migrate!` embeds these files
# into the binary — so a build context without them does not build. Nothing is
# baked into the runtime image: what ships is the binary that carries them.
!migrations
!migrations/**

# The web half's templates, on the same terms: askama expands them into
# rendering code at compile time, so a build context without them does not
# build, and the runtime image carries no HTML file.
!templates
!templates/**

# Not because the release build compiles a test — it compiles none. Cargo.toml
# declares one test target explicitly, and cargo refuses to *parse* a manifest
# whose declared target file is missing, before it has decided which targets to
# build at all. So a context without this directory fails at manifest parsing,
# and `tests/docker_context.rs` is the test that holds the two lists together.
!tests
!tests/**
